```

The Manhattan distance is the sum of the absolute differences in the x- and y-coordinates, so the body of the function implements that formula directly, using the tuple entries at index 0 for the x-coordinate and at index 1 for the y-coordinate. The abs calls convert any negative difference into a positive contribution. Python's equality operator compares floats exactly, so the function returns a float when either coordinate is a float and coincides with the float result the specification requires; for integer coordinates the result is an integer. The three assert statements use only integer coordinates, and the first also lies outside the all-positive corner because one point is at the origin, which can be a safety check that the first term is taken as 0 rather than being mistaken for a degenerate bound on the result.

Verifying an input such as (1.5, -0.3) and (0.2, 3.1) that the tests do not use: the x difference is 1.3 and the y difference is -3.4, whose absolute values add up to 4.7, which manhattan_distance returns. The tuple access needs no indexing keys that differ between input points, so the function will work for any pair of tuples whose two elements hold the coordinates of the respective points.

